+\subsection{Mixed spectra and spectral unmixing}
+
+\subsubsection{Mixing within one collection volume}
+
+One measured spectrum can contain signals from both the sample and its surrounding medium. The collection volume can pass through several regions, each with its own spectrum (Figure~\ref{fig:collection_volume}). Changing their proportions changes the combined spectrum, even if the materials themselves stay the same. Nearby peaks overlap and can appear as one broad or asymmetric peak. Broadening by the instrument makes them harder to separate \citep{bouvet2025}.
+
