-% =========================
-
-\subsection{Computational implementation and reproducibility}
-
-Python will be used to generate spectra, fit the recovery models and calculate errors. Each run will save the concentration field, grid coordinates, frequency-bin edges, collection weights, local response parameters, instrument model, photon budget, random seeds and true contribution distributions. Methods within a comparison will use identical saved inputs. Output metadata will record software versions, solver settings, normalisation, initialisation and stopping criteria, together with any revisions to the initial concentration range or proposed conditions.
-
-Before final comparisons, numerical checks will verify that the local Lorentzian has the stated full-band area, the collection weighting integrates to one, and the fixed-component integral agrees with the analytic mixture. Depth, frequency and concentration discretisations will be refined to check convergence of the reported metrics. The unknown-spectrum analysis will additionally record variation across initialisations rather than reporting only the best-looking solution. The resulting conclusions will concern recovery under the stated simulation assumptions; experimental validation remains outside this study.
-
-
+This is the RMS difference between fitted and expected spectra, divided by the RMS expected spectrum. Both use bin-area units. A small rRMSE with poor contribution recovery means that a method fits the curve well but does not recover how it was formed.
+
+Results will report mean and median errors, their spread and fitting-failure rates across 30 noise realisations. A 95\% bootstrap interval uses 1000 resamples of these realisations, paired across methods. Failed fits include nonfinite values, zero total contributions, constraint errors above $10^{-8}$ or failure to meet the stopping rule. Error summaries use successful fits and report the failure rate alongside them. A fraction error of 0.05 means five percentage points; a normalised Wasserstein error of 0.05 means 5\% of the tested range. Full error distributions will also be reported.
+
+\subsection{Implementation and reproducibility}
+\label{sec:implementation}
+
+The analysis will use NumPy and SciPy for PCA, NNLS and constrained fitting. The VCA implementation in \texttt{brillouin\_decomposition.py} will be checked on mixtures with known components. Code will be kept at \url{https://github.com/Noah2NT/Brillouin_Spectroscopy_Data_Analysis}, with the commit, package versions, settings, random seeds and true spectra recorded for each benchmark. The figures illustrate data generation; the planned recovery comparisons remain to be run. Extending the fixed-sample analysis beyond two components would require further reference spectra or enough information to estimate them.
+
+Together, the tests compare how spectral overlap, photon counts and knowledge of the spectra affect recovery. The error measures distinguish a good fit to the measured curve from a correct estimate of its components. Fitting settings will be fixed after pilot simulations, before the reserved samples are evaluated.
